  <h2>What Lean Body Mass Actually Includes</h2>
  <p>Lean Body Mass (LBM) is everything in your body that is not adipose tissue. It includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skeletal muscle:</strong> The largest component in most adults. The part you build through resistance training.</li>
    <li><strong>Bone mineral content:</strong> The structural mass of your skeleton.</li>
    <li><strong>Organ mass:</strong> Heart, liver, kidneys, brain, and other soft tissues.</li>
    <li><strong>Body water:</strong> Intracellular and extracellular fluid associated with lean tissues.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For most adults, LBM is approximately 60–90% of total body weight, with the higher end in muscular athletes and the lower end in sedentary populations with higher body fat percentage.</p>

  <h2>Why LBM Drives Your Metabolism</h2>
  <p>Resting metabolic rate is determined primarily by fat-free mass. The Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict BMR equations both implicitly weight LBM through height, weight, age, and sex. Per kilogram, lean tissue burns approximately 13 kcal/day at rest, compared with 4.5 kcal/day for adipose tissue. This is why a pound of muscle "burns more calories" than a pound of fat — the difference is real but smaller than often claimed.</p>
  <p>The clinical implications:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Athletes</strong> with high LBM have higher BMRs and can eat more while maintaining weight.</li>
    <li><strong>Older adults</strong> with sarcopenia have lower BMRs and tend to gain fat at constant caloric intake.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight loss</strong> that preserves LBM (via adequate protein and resistance training) is metabolically protective compared to loss that includes significant lean mass.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Boer Equation (1984)</h2>
  <p>The Boer formula estimates LBM from height, weight, and sex:</p>
  <p><strong>Men:</strong> LBM = (0.407 × weight_kg) + (0.267 × height_cm) − 19.2</p>
  <p><strong>Women:</strong> LBM = (0.252 × weight_kg) + (0.473 × height_cm) − 48.3</p>
  <p>For a 175 cm, 75 kg man:</p>
  <p><code>LBM = 30.5 + 46.7 − 19.2 = 58.0 kg</code></p>
  <p>Standard error against DEXA is approximately 3–4 kg. Reasonable accuracy for population estimates and trend tracking; not precise enough for individual clinical decisions.</p>

  <h2>Reference Values</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Typical LBM</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Adult male (average)</td><td>~62 kg</td></tr>
