      <tr><td>Athletic female</td><td>45–55 kg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Elderly male (75 yr)</td><td>50–55 kg</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>LBM in Clinical Contexts</h2>
  <p>Outside of athletics, LBM matters most for two populations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Older adults at risk of sarcopenia.</strong> Loss of LBM accelerates after age 60 and is associated with falls, fractures, dependency, and mortality. Resistance training and adequate protein intake are the primary interventions.</li>
    <li><strong>Patients on GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide, tirzepatide).</strong> Meta-analyses using DEXA show that 20–40% of weight lost via these medications is lean mass. Concurrent resistance training and high-protein intake reduce this loss but cannot eliminate it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use LBM Calculators vs DEXA</h2>
  <p>The Boer formula is a useful estimate for tracking change over time. For clinical decisions where precise body composition matters — chemotherapy dosing, sarcopenia diagnosis, athletic performance optimization — DEXA is the gold standard. Use the calculator for self-monitoring and trend estimation; use DEXA when precision matters.</p>

